  "worked_solution": "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|7x-4|=7x-4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7-\\frac{4}{x})}{(2-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
